# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI coding agents (and humans) working in SampleApp. SampleApp uses Umpire with the VSA pack: Vertical Slice Architecture with one folder for each module.

## Layout

| Path             | Purpose                                                              |
|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `SampleApp.csproj` | The one root project. It compiles `App/` and `Views/` |
| `App/`       | The runtime code. `App/Program.cs` calls `await new SampleAppApp().Run(new WebHost(args))`: it gives the host and its arguments. The app class `SampleAppApp : App<VsaKernel>` names the kernel. It starts the app, and it finds the modules and the views |
| `Views/`     | The Blazor views. Each module has its page in `Views/Pages/`     |
| `App/Features/<Name>/`| One feature module: its module class, `Domain/`, `Infrastructure/`, `Presenters/` and `Slices/<Slice>/` |
| `App/Plugins/<Name>/`| One plugin module: a base of the same layers in each module. It has one folder for each layer, and no `Slices/` |
| `App/Tests/`     | A nested project: one unit test file for each module                |
| `Definition/`    | Optional: a nested project (`Definition/<Name>.Definition.csproj`) with the architecture class of the app, its records and its externals. The app does not reference it |

The app has one root project. A module, a slice or a plugin needs no project: add only its files. A nested project (for example `App/Tests/`) is optional, and the root project does not compile its folder. The folders select the layers, and the rule engine checks each use against the VSA definition after the build.

Each namespace starts with `SampleApp` and follows the folders under `App/`, for example `SampleApp.Features.Billing.Slices.CreateInvoice`. The app class and `Program.cs` keep the namespace `SampleApp.Host` for now (tracker item V15). Put the `using` directives inside the namespace. Then a VSA type wins over a namespace of the app with the same name, for example `App` or `Views`. This is a guideline, not an enforced rule. If the file is inside the namespace of the concept records, put some `using` directives outside the namespace. If that is not enough, add an alias inside the namespace.

Write the input of a request or a command, and the output of its response or result, as record parameters. Each module class registers the services of its module in `protected override void Configure`, for example its repository: `services.AddScoped<IRepository<Order, OrderId>, OrderRepository>();`.

A command goes from one module to another module. It has one handler and one result. A module sends it with `ICommandSender`. The command is `<Name>Command`, its result `<Name>Result` and its handler `<Name>Handler`, in `Slices/<Name>/`.

`Domain/Shared/` is the published domain of a module. Other modules read its types, for example the keys in a command. A type there is immutable and holds data only, with no methods. `UMP020303` checks that it is immutable. The rest of `Domain/` stays private to the module. A published domain uses only its own module. The messages, handlers and presenters of a module can use the published domain of all modules.

A plugin module is for base types of the app, as the framework is the base of each layer: for example a command base in `App/Plugins/Game/Contracts/Commands/GameStep.cs`. A plugin module has the layout of the framework: one folder for each layer, for example `Contracts/Commands/` and `Handlers/Commands/`, and no `Slices/`. Each layer of each module can use the same layer of each plugin module. A plugin module never uses a module. Its module class inherits `Plugin` (`UMP020101`, `UMP020301`). The host configures the plugin modules first, by name, and it loads them through the modules.

## Commands

Use the `umpire` tool to add code. It writes only new files, and it does not write over a file. Write the version of the Umpire packages in `SampleApp.csproj` in place of `<version>`, for example `<PackageReference Include="Umpire.Web" Version="1.2.3" />` gives `1.2.3`.

```bash
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- add module <Name>               # the module class, a status request and a page
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- add request <Name> -m <Module>  # a request, its response and its handler
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- add command <Name> -m <Module>  # a command, its result and its handler
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- add plugin <Name>               # a plugin module with a base command
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- doctor                          # the tool and the Umpire packages have one version
dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<version> -y -- test                            # the tests of each *.Tests project
```

The rule engine checks each type against the VSA definition. The package `Umpire.Engine` of the root project holds the engine, and the package `Umpire` runs the check after each build of the root project. A finding is a build warning. `umpire.check.mode = "Error"` in `.umpireconfig` makes it a build error. An analyzer finding (`UMP01xxxx`) is a warning: `umpire.<ID>.severity = "error"` makes it an error. `-p:UmpireCheck=false` stops the check for one build. The test project does not check the architecture. The check gives `UMP020101` (a use that the definition denies), `UMP020201` (a type that is not the type of its part) and `UMP020202` (a type in a folder that the definition does not have). To change the rules, derive a definition from `VsaArchitecture`, override `Rules(RuleBook)`, and put it in a `Definition/` project. That project references the VSA definition, for example `<PackageReference Include="Umpire.Vsa.Definition" Version="<version>" PrivateAssets="all" />`. The check finds the definition in `Definition/`, and the app does not name it.

`.umpireconfig` is TOML. The top-level keys are the rule values, for example `umpire.UMP010101.max = 140`. A table with the name of a module holds its settings, for example `[Notebook]`. A string needs quotes.

All build output goes to `artifacts~/` in the app folder, the folder of `.umpireconfig`. No `bin/` or `obj/` folder is next to the code. To get a clean build, delete `artifacts~/`.

The Umpire packages come from GitHub Packages. Set `GITHUB_TOKEN` before a restore, for example to the output of `gh auth token`.

To update Umpire, run `update` with the new version of the tool, for example `dotnet tool exec Umpire.Cli@<new version> -y -- update`. It changes the version on the first line of each project and in each `Umpire.*` package reference.

## Core principles

- **Organize by slice, not by layer.** Each slice is one folder, `Slices/<Slice>/`, with its request, response, events and handler. A slice has one kind of handler.
- **Modules are independent.** A module uses another module only through its contracts and its published domain.
- **Keep shared code small.** Share code only when two or more slices need it.
- **No comments in C# code** (`UMP010106`). Put the information in a name: a type, a member, a variable or a test.
